---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Intuition Guide - Replenish Guide"
>
    <PageHeader 
        title="The Intuition Guide"
        description="<strong>A few tips:</strong> Intuition is a skill-intensive card, and it might take some time to learn all the possible lines that the card enables, as it plays multiple critical roles throughout a game. A fundamental rule of Intuition is the tradeoff between immediacy and future availability: when you tutor for a non-combo piece (like removal or a sideboard card), you are effectively removing three copies of that card from your deck to guarantee access to one. Keep in mind also when you are building your deck that any card you intend to reliably fetch with Intuition requires you to run at least 3 copies in your 75."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/intuition.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Intuition"
        imageCaption="Intuition by April Lee"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <Card title="General Heuristics" titleTag="h2">
    <p>
        Timing is everything. Because Intuition is an instant, you almost always
        want to cast it on your opponent's end step. This lets you untap with
        all your mana available to slam a Replenish or hold up interaction on
        your turn, giving your opponent a much smaller window to react. This
        also gives you more information to make better decisions, and that is
        always good.
    </p>
    <p>
        Dealing with incomplete piles requires creativity—if you desperately
        need a Swords to Plowshares, but you've already drawn one and only have
        two left in your library, you'll need to build a hybrid pile like Swords
        + Swords + Parallax Wave. Always keep track of what's left in your deck
        so you don't cast Intuition only to realize you can't fetch three of a
        kind.
    </p>
    <p>
        Finally, when playing through graveyard hate like Tormod's Crypt or
        Planar Void post-sideboard, Intuition stops being your combo setup tool
        and immediately pivots to being your best out for finding answers (like
        fetching 3 copies of Seal of Cleansing to clear the hate before filling
        the graveyard).
    </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Common Scenarios & Piles" titleTag="h2">
    <p>Here are the most common scenarios and how to navigate them:</p>

    <ul style="padding-left: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Dumping Combo Pieces:</strong><br />
            The primary use. You can use it to fetch the exact enchantments you need
            into the graveyard. This is perfect when you keep a hand with Replenish
            plus a discard outlet (like Attunement or Frantic Search). <em
                >Crucial Note:</em
            > Since the opponent chooses which card goes to your hand, if you absolutely
            want a specific enchantment in the graveyard, you must pick at least two
            copies of it in your pile.<br />
            <strong>Examples:</strong> Opal + Decree + Wave, Opal + Opal + Wave, Opal
            + Opal + Decree, Wave x3 (against aggro).
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong
                >Tutoring Removal (Swords to Plowshares or Seal of Cleansing):</strong
            ><br />
            Used when you face an immediate, game-ending threat. Getting Seal of Cleansing
            is slightly better than Swords because you can always bring the discarded
            Seals back to the battlefield later with a Replenish.<br />
            <strong>Examples:</strong> Swords to Plowshares x3, Seal of Cleansing
            x3.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Guaranteeing Land Drops:</strong><br />
            You can search for 3 lands to fix your colors, guarantee you don't miss
            a critical land drop, or even accelerate your mana a turn early by grabbing
            3 Ancient Tombs. It's a less frequent play, but highly impactful when
            you need it.<br />
            <strong>Examples:</strong> Ancient Tomb x3, Skycloud expanse x3, Plains
            x3.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Finding a Discard Outlet:</strong><br />
            When you have all the combo pieces and Replenish in your hand but no way
            to discard them, you can Intuition for 3 Frantic Searches or 3 Attunements.
            <em>Note:</em> Grabbing 3 Attunements is a very slow, mana-intensive play,
            so make sure you have the time to execute it.<br />
            <strong>Examples:</strong> Frantic Search x3, Attunement x3.
        </li>
        <li style="margin-bottom: 1rem;">
            <strong>Finding Sideboard Silver Bullets:</strong><br />
            You can fetch 3 copies of a key sideboard card—like Meddling Mage or Abeyance—to
            stop an opposing combo or fight through the mirror match.<br />
            <strong>Examples:</strong> Meddling Mage x3, Abeyance x3, Tsabo's web
            x3.
        </li>
    </ul>
    </Card>
</Layout>
